MT940 has no comment syntax, and a bank's covering text stands before the
first tag exactly like this — which is why the reader drops everything above
the first tag instead of refusing the file.

A month of a German business current account, in the shape a German bank
writes MT940: structured :86: subfields (?00 posting text, ?2n remittance in
27-character slices, ?31 the counterparty's account, ?32/?33 their name).

It is deliberately the SAME month as camt053_de_january.xml, transaction for
transaction, with the same bank references — because the two formats must
stage to the same lines, and bank_import_tenancy.rs proves it by importing
both and finding four duplicates and nothing new.

Hand-authored to the published shape rather than copied from a bank's own
file: a real statement is somebody's money. The IBANs are the standard test
numbers used in the specifications' own examples.

Opening + the four transactions = closing, and the golden suite asserts it.

